\ sod16.4 - make the Forth compiler emit SOD16 tokens instead of cells.
\
\ Everything here is the same as cpt16.4 except the three lines that
\ make a call, and those three lines are why this file is twice the
\ size. That is the finding, not an accident of implementation.
\
\ SOD16 names a call by WORD NUMBER: token 256+n means "the body of the
\ nth word ever defined", and the engine resolves it through a table it
\ builds at load by walking the dictionary chain. Two consequences fall
\ out the moment the system has to compile for itself:
\
\   1. The compiler needs the reverse map. It has an address - the thing
\      `'` and NAME> give it - and it must produce a number. The engine's
\      table is number -> address and lives in the engine's malloc'd
\      memory, not the image's. So the image has to build its own copy,
\      and search it, on every call it compiles.
\
\   2. A word defined after load has no entry at all. The table was
\      sized to the words that were there and is never grown. So there
\      must be an escape - FARCALL, opcode 72 - that names an address
\      directly, and every call to newly compiled code uses it.
\
\ CPT16 deletes the table and computes the target arithmetically, and
\ both problems disappear with it: see cpt16.4's CALL,, which is one
\ line. The comparison between these two files is the argument.

\ ---- opcode numbers -------------------------------------------------
  0 CONSTANT NOOP-OP
  1 CONSTANT EXIT-OP
  2 CONSTANT LIT16-OP
  3 CONSTANT BRANCH-OP
  4 CONSTANT 0BRANCH-OP
 68 CONSTANT LIT32-OP
 69 CONSTANT DOVAR-OP
 70 CONSTANT DODOES-OP
 71 CONSTANT LIT64-OP
 72 CONSTANT FARCALL-OP
 73 CONSTANT DODOESF-OP

\ ---- raw emission ---------------------------------------------------
: OP, ( n --- )
  DUP 255 AND C, 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, ;

: W@ ( a-addr --- n )
  DUP C@ SWAP 1+ C@ 8 LSHIFT OR ;

: !W ( n a-addr --- )
  2DUP C! 1+ SWAP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND SWAP C! ;

\ ---- the word-number table ------------------------------------------
\ Rebuilt in the image, because the engine's copy is not reachable from
\ Forth. It is the same walk the engine does at load: follow the link
\ chain to the oldest word, then number coming back, so entry 0 is the
\ first word ever defined.
\
\ Body addresses ascend with word number - the dictionary only grows
\ upwards - so the array is sorted and can be searched in log n rather
\ than scanned. That matters: this runs once per call compiled.

VARIABLE SOD16-TAB ( --- a-addr )   \ 0 until built
VARIABLE SOD16-N   ( --- a-addr )   \ number of words in the array
VARIABLE SOD16-CUT ( --- a-addr )   \ first address NOT eligible
VARIABLE SOD16-KEY ( --- a-addr )
VARIABLE SOD16-NFA ( --- a-addr )
VARIABLE SOD16-TA  ( --- a-addr )

: SOD16-HERE ( --- a-addr )
\ The return address of whoever called this: the address of the token
\ just after that call.
  R@ ;

: SOD16-MARK ( --- a-addr )
\ This word's OWN body address, found without naming it. `[']` cannot be
\ used: it compiles an absolute xt of the cell session, which the
\ translator does not relocate.
  SOD16-HERE 2 - ;

\ SOD16-MARK is deliberately defined HERE, near the top of the overlay,
\ and its body address is the cutoff: only words older than it are given
\ numbers. That covers all of kernel.4, which is what matters, and
\ excludes this file's own words and everything compiled later.
\
\ Excluding a word is always SAFE, and that is the property the whole
\ scheme rests on. Numbers run oldest-first, so leaving late words out
\ does not renumber the early ones; an excluded target simply gets the
\ far form instead of the compact one.
\
\ The first version instead took the cutoff to be the definition being
\ compiled, on the theory that everything above it was new. That is only
\ true if the table is built during the FIRST runtime definition, and it
\ is built during the first definition that contains a CALL - which in
\ the test corpus was the third. Two runtime words were handed numbers
\ 319 and up, past the end of a table with 319 entries, and the engine
\ jumped through whatever followed it in memory.

: NEXT-NFA ( nfa --- nfa' )
\ The previous word in the chain, or 0 at the end. The link is stored
\ RELATIVE to its own cell, exactly as the engine reads it.
  1 CELLS - DUP @ DUP 0= IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN + ;

: SOD16-COLLECT ( --- )
\ Every eligible word, from every thread, in whatever order the chains
\ give them. The word list is HASHED, so one chain no longer reaches
\ the whole dictionary and no longer yields address order either.
  0 SOD16-N !
  FORTH-WORDLIST @ 1+ 1 DO
    FORTH-WORDLIST I CELLS + @ SOD16-NFA !
    BEGIN SOD16-NFA @ WHILE
      SOD16-NFA @ NAME> DUP SOD16-CUT @ U< IF
        SOD16-TAB @ SOD16-N @ CELLS + !
        1 SOD16-N +!
      ELSE DROP THEN
      SOD16-NFA @ NEXT-NFA SOD16-NFA !
    REPEAT
  LOOP ;

: SOD16-SWAPC ( a --- )
\ Exchange the cells at a and a+CELL.
  SOD16-TA !
  SOD16-TA @ @ SOD16-TA @ 1 CELLS + @
  SOD16-TA @ ! SOD16-TA @ 1 CELLS + ! ;

: SOD16-SORT ( --- )
\ Ascending by body address, which IS definition order - the dictionary
\ only grows upwards. The engine's loader sorts the same way, and that
\ sort is now the definition of a word number that both ends agree on.
\ It used to fall out of walking the single chain backwards.
  SOD16-N @ 2 < IF EXIT THEN
  SOD16-N @ 1- 0 DO
    SOD16-N @ 1- 0 DO
      SOD16-TAB @ I CELLS +
      DUP @ OVER 1 CELLS + @ > IF SOD16-SWAPC ELSE DROP THEN
    LOOP
  LOOP ;

: SOD16-BUILD ( --- )
\ Build the array once, on the heap - never with ALLOT, which is called
\ in the middle of compiling a definition and would insert the table
\ into the body of the word being defined.
  SOD16-MARK SOD16-CUT !
  8192 CELLS ALLOCATE ABORT" sod16: cannot allocate the word table"
  SOD16-TAB !
  SOD16-COLLECT SOD16-SORT ;

: SOD16-NUM ( body --- n -1 | body 0 )
\ The word number of the body at `body`, if it has one.
  SOD16-TAB @ 0= IF SOD16-BUILD THEN
  DUP SOD16-KEY !
  0 SOD16-N @                            ( body lo hi )
  BEGIN 2DUP U< WHILE
    2DUP + 2/                            ( body lo hi mid )
    DUP CELLS SOD16-TAB @ + @ SOD16-KEY @ U<
    IF 1+ ROT DROP SWAP ELSE SWAP DROP THEN
  REPEAT
  DROP                                   ( body lo )
  DUP SOD16-N @ U< IF
    DUP CELLS SOD16-TAB @ + @ SOD16-KEY @ =
  ELSE 0 THEN                            ( body lo flag )
  IF SWAP DROP -1 ELSE DROP 0 THEN ;

\ ---- literals -------------------------------------------------------
: LIT, ( n --- )
  DUP 65536 U< IF LIT16-OP OP, OP, EXIT THEN
  1 CELLS 4 = IF LIT32-OP OP, DUP OP, 16 RSHIFT OP, EXIT THEN
  DUP 2147483648 + 4294967296 U< IF
    LIT32-OP OP, DUP OP, 16 RSHIFT OP, EXIT
  THEN
  LIT64-OP OP,
  DUP OP, DUP 16 RSHIFT OP, DUP 32 RSHIFT OP, 48 RSHIFT OP, ;

: LITERAL16 ( n --- )
  LIT, ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- calls ----------------------------------------------------------
: CALL, ( a-addr --- )
\ One token if the target has a word number, three if it does not.
\ Compare cpt16.4, where this is a subtract and a shift with no lookup
\ and no second form.
  SOD16-NUM IF
    256 + OP,
  ELSE
    START @ - FARCALL-OP OP, DUP OP, 16 RSHIFT OP,
  THEN ;

: COMPILE,16 ( xt --- )
  DUP >NAME C@ 32 AND IF
    W@ OP,
  ELSE
    CALL,
  THEN ;

: ;16 ( --- )
  EXIT-OP OP, 0 STATE ! ?CSP REVEAL ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- control flow ---------------------------------------------------
: >MARK ( --- a-addr )
  HERE 0 OP, ;

: >RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
  DUP HERE SWAP - 2/ SWAP !W ;

: <RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
  HERE - 2/ OP, ;

: BEGIN16 ( --- a-addr )
  HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: UNTIL16 ( a-addr --- )
  0BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: AGAIN16 ( a-addr --- )
  BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: IF16 ( --- a-addr )
  0BRANCH-OP OP, >MARK ; IMMEDIATE

: THEN16 ( a-addr --- )
  >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: ELSE16 ( a-addr1 --- a-addr2 )
  BRANCH-OP OP, >MARK SWAP >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: WHILE16 ( a-addr1 --- a-addr2 a-addr1 )
  POSTPONE IF16 SWAP ; IMMEDIATE

: REPEAT16 ( a-addr1 a-addr2 --- )
  BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- defining words -------------------------------------------------
: CREATE16 ( "ccc" --- )
  HEADER REVEAL DOVAR-OP OP, ALIGN ;

: VARIABLE16 ( "ccc" --- )
  CREATE16 0 , ;

: CONSTANT16 ( x "ccc" --- )
  HEADER REVEAL LIT, EXIT-OP OP, ;

: RECURSE16 ( --- )
\ Always the far form: the word being defined is by definition not in
\ the table.
  LAST @ NAME> START @ - FARCALL-OP OP, DUP OP, 16 RSHIFT OP, ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- DO ... LOOP ----------------------------------------------------
: OPERAND-ALIGN ( --- )
\ (LOOP) and friends are load-time words, so the call that precedes the
\ inline operand is always the one-token form.
  BEGIN HERE 2 + 1 CELLS 1- AND WHILE NOOP-OP OP, REPEAT ;

: DO16 ( --- a-addr )
  POSTPONE (DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 0 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: LOOP16 ( a-addr --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: +LOOP16 ( a-addr --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (+LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: ?DO16 ( --- a-addr )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (?DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 'LEAVE ! 0 , HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: LEAVE16 ( --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LEAVE) HERE 'LEAVE @ , 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- DOES> -----------------------------------------------------------
\ CREATE16 laid down [DOVAR][pad to a cell], so there is exactly one
\ cell to overwrite. A load-time tail fits as [DODOES][token]; a tail in
\ newly compiled code needs the address, which is [DODOESF][lo][hi] -
\ six bytes, which still fits an 8-byte cell and, at 4-byte cells, is
\ the reason DODOESF exists at all rather than reusing FARCALL.

: (;CODE)16 ( --- )
  R> ALIGNED LAST @ NAME>                ( tail word )
  OVER SOD16-NUM IF                      ( tail word n )
    ROT DROP                             ( word n )
    OVER DODOES-OP SWAP !W
    SWAP 2 + !W
  ELSE                                   ( tail word tail )
    DROP                                 ( tail word )
    DUP DODOESF-OP SWAP !W               ( tail word )
    2 + SWAP START @ - 2/                ( addr off/2 )
    DUP 65536 U< 0= ABORT" sod16: DOES> tail beyond 128 KB"
    SWAP !W
  THEN ;

: DOES>16 ( --- )
  POSTPONE (;CODE) ALIGN POSTPONE R> ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- debugging aid --------------------------------------------------
: TOKENS. ( a-addr n --- )
  0 DO DUP I 2 * + W@ . LOOP DROP CR ;
